drafts.P8093:
ANR started the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission (FERC) pre-filing process in early 2026. A formal application was expected in late 2026 and a certificate in late 2027. {b01tce}

The project would loop the ANR mainline with 36-inch pipe, add one 4,700 horsepower compressor station in Shawano County, Wisconsin, and add two meter stations. EIA lists about 390 million cubic feet per day (MMcf/d) of added capacity and an in-service date of November 1, 2029. {b01eia}

TC Energy approved the project in May 2025 at about $900 million. {b01dcd} The added capacity is tied to 20-year take-or-pay contracts with Midwest utilities that serve gas-fired power plants. The plants are meant to meet growing data center demand, but no single data center is named. {b01dcd}

drafts.P8299:
A Wisconsin agricultural impact statement from March 2021 describes a We Energies proposal for about 46 miles of new 24-inch pipe in Walworth, Kenosha and Racine Counties. {b01dat} A news report from April 2021 said We Energies had approvals from the Wisconsin Public Service Commission and the Department of Natural Resources. It said work began in 2020, the cost was $186 million, and completion was expected by the end of 2021. {b01mc}

An earlier newspaper article said the line would start at the Bluff Creek Gate Station in La Grange and end in Mount Pleasant. The route then favored was estimated at $174.1 million. {b01gaz}

The commission's case page for the Paris engine plant places seven gas engines, up to 128 megawatts, at the Lakeshore Capacity Improvement Project regulator station in the Town of Paris, Kenosha County. {b01psc} A Milwaukee public radio report on the May 2025 approval said We Energies points to a Microsoft data center being built in Racine County. {b01wuw}

drafts.P8146:
Elba Express Company, a Kinder Morgan company, proposed the Bridge Project to serve a new gas-fired power plant in Colleton County, South Carolina, and growing residential and commercial demand.{a2_5} Santee Cooper and Dominion Energy contracted Kinder Morgan to build, own and operate the $431 million pipeline.{a2_7} The utilities planned a 2,200-megawatt plant on the site of a former coal plant near Canadys.{a2_7} South Carolina utility regulators approved the plant in May 2026.{a2_8}

In October 2025, Kinder Morgan sent letters to 185 South Carolina property owners asking for permission to survey their land.{a2_7} The company said the project was supported by long-term contracts with customers.{a2_6} Its fact sheet gave a schedule of Federal Energy Regulatory Commission pre-filing in the second quarter of 2026, an application in the first quarter of 2027, construction in the first quarter of 2029 and service in the second quarter of 2030.{a2_5}

In May 2026, utility executives called the plant essential to meeting the state's power needs amid rapid population and industrial growth. The report said that growth included a ballooning number of energy-intensive data centers.{a2_8}

drafts.P8135:
Howard Energy Partners and Kinder Morgan announced the project on July 12, 2023. Dos Caminos, a joint venture of Howard Energy Partners and an affiliate of Eagle Ford Midstream, was building about 62 miles of 36-inch pipe. The project also included compression, treating and dehydration. The line ran from near Howard's facilities in Webb County to the Kinder Morgan Texas Pipeline compressor station at Freer. Howard's chief executive said construction of the Spears expansion project had begun.{a3_5}

Completion was planned for the fourth quarter of 2023, together with a 67-mile 42-inch Kinder Morgan Tejas line to Sinton. Together the two projects would deliver up to 2 billion cubic feet per day to Gulf Coast markets. Customers named were power generators, industrial customers and liquefied natural gas exporters.{a3_5}{a3_6}

The U.S. Energy Information Administration lists the Spears Expansion Project as completed on November 10, 2023. It gives the length as 51 miles, not the 62 miles announced.{a3_7}

drafts.P8169:
Great Lakes Gas Transmission applied to the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission on April 4, 1996, under docket CP96-297. It sought three 36-inch loops totaling 24.5 miles in Delta and Mackinac counties, Michigan. The loops measured 2.4, 12.5 and 9.6 miles. They were the only unlooped parts of the 968-mile 36-inch mainline. The company aimed to complete the looping for reliability and operating flexibility.{a3_9}

The first loop ran from milepost 560.3 to the Rapid River compressor station at milepost 562.7. The second ran from milepost 562.7 to milepost 575.2. The third ran from the Naubinway compressor station at milepost 640.1 to milepost 649.7. The work also included changes at the Rapid River and Naubinway compressor stations and the Rapid River meter station.{a3_9}

The U.S. Energy Information Administration lists the project as completed in November 1998 at a cost of $33.4 million.{a3_7}

Great Lakes built a second expansion in the same period, the 1998 System Wide Expansion. It added looping in Minnesota and Wisconsin.{a3_10}

drafts.P8170:
The Federal Energy Regulatory Commission issued an environmental assessment notice on August 15, 1997, under docket CP96-647. The plan was three 36-inch loops totaling about 71.5 miles. The first loop was 22.0 miles in Kittson County, Minnesota. The second was 26.7 miles in Clearwater, Beltrami and Hubbard counties, Minnesota. The third was 22.8 miles in Carlton County, Minnesota, and Douglas County, Wisconsin.{a3_10}

The plan also added two compressor units of 7,400 horsepower at St. Vincent, Minnesota, and Farwell, Michigan. The purpose was an added 126,000 thousand cubic feet per day of firm transportation between Emerson, Minnesota, and St. Clair, Michigan.{a3_10}

The U.S. Energy Information Administration lists the expansion as completed in November 1998 at a cost of $122.8 million.{a3_7}

drafts.P8183:
Islander East Pipeline Company filed with the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission in June 2001. It proposed about 50 miles of interstate pipeline to serve Connecticut, New York City and Long Island. Subsidiaries of Duke Energy and KeySpan owned it equally. The company expected an in-service date of 2003.{a3_11}

The commission made a preliminary determination in January 2002 for 260,000 dekatherms per day, enough for 600,000 homes. A companion Algonquin proposal went with it.{a3_12} The commission issued the certificate in September 2002. The Algonquin project would upgrade 13 miles and add a compressor station at Cheshire, Connecticut.{a3_13}

Connecticut had found that the line would harm shellfish beds and water quality under the Coastal Zone Management Act. In May 2004 the Commerce Secretary overruled that finding. The Islander East portion was to cost $150 million and the Algonquin portion $30 million, with up to 400 million cubic feet per day.{a3_14}

In September 2007 a federal court in Bridgeport, Connecticut, set aside the Commerce decision as arbitrary and capricious. The sponsors were then Spectra Energy and KeySpan. The $180 million project would deliver 285,000 dekatherms per day, with about 22 miles on the floor of Long Island Sound and a lateral to Calverton. That article gave the length as 45 miles. The Connecticut attorney general said the ruling should end the proposal. Whether the sponsors would appeal was not known. No report of construction has been found.{a3_15}

drafts.P8231:
Petal Gas Storage filed an application on the project under Federal Energy Regulatory Commission docket CP01-69, noticed on February 5, 2001. It proposed 58.7 miles of new bidirectional 36-inch pipe from its storage header in Forrest County through Jones and Jasper counties, Mississippi. The line would end at proposed interconnects with Southern Natural Gas and Destin Pipeline near Enterprise in Clarke County. The plan also called for a 9,000 horsepower compressor station near Heidelberg in Jasper County and 0.3 miles of line to a Transco interconnect. The cost was about $94.3 million.{a3_20}

Southern Company Services signed a 20-year firm transportation precedent agreement for the full 700,000 thousand cubic feet per day.{a3_20}

The U.S. Energy Information Administration lists the project as completed in July 2002.{a3_7}

drafts.P8276:
Energy Transfer Partners announced the Carthage Loop in 2008. It was to expand the new 42-inch Cleburne to Carthage pipeline from the Houston Pipe Line interconnect at Texoma to Carthage. The company planned 32 miles of 42-inch pipe and 500 million cubic feet per day of added capacity. Construction was expected to begin in June 2008, with service by September 2008. Producer commitments came from XTO Energy, Chesapeake Energy, Quicksilver Resources and Gastar Exploration.{a3_22}

Energy Transfer said it completed the loop in September 2008. The line runs from Minden to Carthage, Texas, and connects to the company's intrastate network and the Carthage Hub. The company said its planned Texas Independence Pipeline would connect to the loop.{a3_21}

The U.S. Energy Information Administration lists completion on September 10, 2008, at a cost of $94 million.{a3_7}

drafts.P8152:
Enterprise announced the line in August 2008 as a lateral of about 40 miles, 30 to 36 inches in diameter, running from the Trinity River Basin to the Sherman Extension near Justin, Texas.{a6_3} It would have given Barnett Shale producers in Tarrant and Denton counties up to 1 Bcf/d of takeaway. Long-term producer agreements anchored the project, and Enterprise targeted the third quarter of 2009.{a6_3}

The plan also included a line for Newark East field gas in Wise County. The Sherman Extension has 1.1 Bcf/d of capacity, and 900 MMcf/d of it was signed up at the time.{a6_3}

The Energy Information Administration lists the project as completed on July 28, 2010.{a6_eia}

drafts.P8278:
Transco proposed three 42-inch loops under FERC docket CP09-57. A FERC environmental assessment notice in June 2009 described the Coosa Loop at 4.40 miles in Coosa County, Alabama, the Cowpens Loop at 9.39 miles in Spartanburg and Cherokee counties, South Carolina, and the Iredell Loop at 8.27 miles in Iredell and Rowan counties, North Carolina.{a6_18}

Williams Partners said in July 2010 that the first phase, a compression addition, had gone into service for Constellation's plant in Tallapoosa County, Alabama. The second phase would add 218,500 dekatherms per day by summer 2011 for generating plants in North Carolina. It requires about 22 miles of 42-inch pipe and a new 20,500 horsepower compressor facility in South Carolina.{a6_19}

The Energy Information Administration lists the project as completed on April 27, 2011, at a cost of 148 million dollars.{a6_eia}

drafts.P8279:
FERC approved the Dalton Expansion in 2016. Williams Partners planned construction for the third quarter of 2016 and completion in 2017. The project includes pipe of 16 to 30 inches and a compressor facility in Carroll County, Georgia.{a6_20} Construction began in August 2016.{a6_22}

Williams Partners and Southern Company Gas, which co-own the project, placed it in service in July 2017. It delivers gas to Oglethorpe Power's generating facility, Atlanta Gas Light and the City of Cartersville, with delivery points in Paulding and Murray counties.{a6_21} The addition raised Transco's system design capacity to 14.2 MMdt/d.{a6_22}

The Energy Information Administration lists the project as completed in April 2017, at a cost of 471.9 million dollars.{a6_eia}

drafts.P8119:
Columbia proposed the loops as part of the Hardy Storage Project with Hardy Storage Company LLC. That project would also add storage wells, well lines and a compressor station in West Virginia. {a7_4}

The Federal Energy Regulatory Commission staff environmental assessment of August 2005 covered three new 24-inch loops totaling about 30.2 miles in Shenandoah, Rockingham, Page and Greene counties, Virginia. It also covered a 2.6-mile 14-inch loop in Louisa County. {a7_4}

The project was completed in April 2007 at a reported cost of $55 million. {a7_eia}

drafts.P8120:
Columbia was under a U.S. Department of Transportation mandate to replace deteriorated sections of the line. The project would abandon 43.4 miles of 14-inch pipe and replace it with 20-inch pipe, mostly in the existing right-of-way. {a7_5}

The work was to be done in two phases, in 2005 and 2006. It would not increase deliverable volumes. {a7_5}

The project was completed in November 2006. {a7_eia}

drafts.P8122:
The Federal Energy Regulatory Commission staff issued an environmental assessment on August 5, 2026 under docket CP26-19. It covers about 30.2 miles of new pipeline, of which 24.62 miles is 36-inch pipe, and about 48.5 miles of existing pipe would be abandoned. {a7_6}

The stated purpose is to replace vintage 1950s pipe for safety, integrity and reliability. Comments on the assessment were due August 31, 2026. {a7_6}

A TC Energy fact sheet said the company intended to file with the commission in November 2025. It said construction was expected to start in the first quarter of 2027, with service in the fourth quarter of 2027. {a7_7}

drafts.P8214:
The project replaced the existing 268-mile 26-inch line from Sumas to Washougal after a U.S. Department of Transportation Corrective Action Order. Abandonment was to be completed by December 31, 2006. {a7_15}

Two breaks in 2003 led the Office of Pipeline Safety to issue Corrective Action Orders and the segment was idled. The Federal Energy Regulatory Commission issued a certificate in September 2005. {a7_16}

The project added about 80 miles of new 36-inch pipe and 10,760 hp of compression at two stations. By December 2006 all facilities were complete, at a final cost of about $325 million. {a7_16}

drafts.P8127:
The Coos County Board of Commissioners applied to the Bureau of Land Management on May 17, 2000, for a right-of-way for a 12-inch gas pipeline from a Williams Gas Pipeline meter facility near Roseburg to Coos Bay. Smaller 4-inch and 6-inch lines were to branch off the main line to serve Coquille and Myrtle Point, and perhaps Bandon later. On November 19, 2002, the bureau published a notice opening a 30-day review of the final environmental impact statement.{a8_3}

In July 1999, Natural Gas Intelligence reported that the line was expected to cost about $45 million. The Oregon legislature had set aside $4 million. Another $20 million depended on county voters approving a bond issue.{a8_4}

The U.S. Energy Information Administration lists the project as completed on December 31, 2004, at a cost of $28 million.{a8_5}

drafts.P8176:
Virginia Natural Gas broke ground on the Hampton Roads Crossing on May 12, 2008. The line joined two parts of its system that had been separate, on the north and south sides of Hampton Roads harbor. The company said the link would add reliability for residential and industrial customers and for military facilities in the area. It estimated the cost at $123 million and slated completion for late 2009. Directional drilling was to be used for 75 percent of the harbor crossing, and 90 percent of the onshore route was to follow existing municipal or utility right-of-way.{a8_8}

A Virginia state history page says the crossing linked the south side, supplied from the Columbia Gas Transmission pipeline, with the Peninsula side, supplied by Dominion Transmission. It says the networks were joined in 2010. It says the crossing did not increase the gas available in Hampton Roads but gave the utility more flexibility to maintain pressure.{a8_7}

A pipe supplier said the project used six marine directional drilling segments. It described the longest, 7,357 feet under the Elizabeth River, as a record.{a8_9}

drafts.P8257:
Southern Star filed an application on January 21, 2026, in Federal Energy Regulatory Commission docket CP26-74 for a certificate under Section 7(c) of the Natural Gas Act. The Viola Project would add Line UA, a compressor station, a meter station and a flow station. It would supply the 710-megawatt Viola combined-cycle power plant, then under construction in Sumner County and to be owned and operated by Evergy Kansas Central, Inc.{a8_15}

The project would connect to the existing 20-inch Line U. The new Wellington Compressor Station would have three compressor units totaling 12,375 horsepower. FERC opened a scoping period in March 2026 with comments due April 3, 2026.{a8_16} A notice published April 13, 2026, set issuance of the environmental assessment for October 9, 2026. FERC published a notice of availability of the assessment on October 15, 2026.{a8_15}

The U.S. Energy Information Administration lists the project at $117.8 million, with an in-service year of 2028.{a8_5}

drafts.P8315:
Crossroads Pipeline Company, owned by TC Energy, posted a non-binding open season on February 9, 2026. The open season tested interest in adding up to 1.5 Bcf/d of capacity by the fourth quarter of 2030. The existing Crossroads line carries 300 MMcf/d.{b10_3}

The expansion would loop the mainline and add infrastructure on TC Energy's existing system. It would add deliveries to the Joliet hub near Chicago and to points on Northern Border Pipeline between Illinois and South Dakota. It would also add a receipt point with Rover Pipeline in Defiance, Ohio.{b10_3}

The U.S. Energy Information Administration listed the project as announced, with a target in-service date of November 1, 2030 that depends on binding commitments. It lists the demand served as power and data centers. No data center customer is named.{b10_2}

drafts.P8143:
East Tennessee Natural Gas filed a FERC application for the Patriot Project in 2001. In July 2001 the company said it would more than double the planned capacity of the extension to 510 million cubic feet per day, at a cost of $289 million. Seven customers had committed to 87 percent of delivery capacity. The company expected construction to start in July 2002.{b13_8}

FERC authorized the project in November 2002. The extension was to run about 94 miles of 24 inch pipe from Wythe County, Virginia, through Carroll, Patrick and Henry counties to Rockingham County, North Carolina, to supply new power plants.{b13_9} Construction began in April 2003 with a horizontal drill under the New River in Wythe County.{b13_10}

In January 2009, East Tennessee asked FERC to vacate part of the 2002 authorization, including a 7.04 mile lateral. The company said two planned power plants in Virginia would not be built. It also said it had finished the facilities needed to serve firm shippers.{b13_11}

drafts.P8321:
National Fuel Gas Supply Corporation filed a prior notice request with FERC on August 29, 2025, under docket CP25-541-000, using its blanket certificate. The request covered a meter and regulator station and a new connection to the Shippingport Power Station in Beaver and Mercer counties, Pennsylvania. The estimated cost was $57.3 million, and the line would give 205,000 dekatherms per day of added firm service from Line N.{b13_17}

In 2026 National Fuel said the lateral would serve demand tied to a behind-the-meter data center, and that the developer had contracted for all of the lateral's capacity under a long-term agreement. A September 2026 report said construction began in March 2026 and service was targeted for late 2026.{b13_18}

The data center is Project Phoenix, a 2 gigawatt campus that Aligned Data Centers planned at the Shippingport site. The borough approved its conditional-use application on March 31, 2026, and Aligned broke ground on September 10, 2026. Aligned said it would use onsite natural-gas fuel cells instead of the grid.{b13_18}
